"subprocess — Subprocess management — Python 3.14.8 documentation (https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/subprocess.html)\nciteturn60view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn59view1\",\"pattern\":\"start_new_session\"}); Total lines: 920\nL200: class subprocess.Popen(args, bufsize=-1, executable=None, stdin=None, stdout=None, stderr=None, preexec_fn=None, close_fds=True, shell=False, cwd=None, env=None, universal_newlines=None, startupinfo=None, creationflags=0, restore_signals=True, start_new_session=False, pass_fds=(), * (Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)), group=None, extra_groups=None, user=None, umask=-1, encoding=None, errors=None, text=None, pipesize=-1, process_group=None)cite8†¶ L259: Additionally, stderr can be cite12†`STDOUT` , which indicates that the stderr data from the applications should be captured into the same file handle as for stdout.\nL260: If preexec_fn is set to a callable object, this object will be called in the child process just before the child is executed. (POSIX only)\nL261: \nL262: Warning\nL263: \nL264: The preexec_fn parameter is NOT SAFE to use in the presence of threads in your application. The child process could deadlock before exec is called.\nL265: \nL266: Note\nL267: If you need to modify the environment for the child use the env parameter rather than doing it in a preexec_fn. The start_new_session and process_group parameters should take the place of code using preexec_fn to call cite62†`os.setsid()` or cite63†`os.setpgid()` in the child.\nL268: Changed in version 3.8: The preexec_fn parameter is no longer supported in subinterpreters. The use of the parameter in a subinterpreter raises cite64†`RuntimeError` . The new restriction may affect applications that are deployed in mod_wsgi, uWSGI, and other embedded environments.\nL269: If close_fds is true, all file descriptors except `0`, `1` and `2` will be closed before the child process is executed. Otherwise when close_fds is false, file descriptors obey their inheritable flag as described in cite65†Inheritance of File Descriptors .\nL270: \nL271: On Windows, if close_fds is true then no handles will be inherited by the child process unless explicitly passed in the `handle_list` element of cite66†`STARTUPINFO.lpAttributeList` , or by standard handle redirection.\nL272: Changed in version 3.2: The default for close_fds was changed from cite67†`False` to what is described above.\nL273: \nL274: Changed in version 3.7: On Windows the default for close_fds was changed from cite67†`False` to cite40†`True` when redirecting the standard handles. It’s now possible to set close_fds to `True` when redirecting the standard handles.\nL275: \nL276: pass_fds is an optional sequence of file descriptors to keep open between the parent and child. Providing any pass_fds forces close_fds to be cite40†`True` . (POSIX only)\nL277: Changed in version 3.2: The pass_fds parameter was added.\nL278: \nL279: If cwd is not `None`, the function changes the working directory to cwd before executing the child. cwd can be a string, bytes or cite56†path-like object. On POSIX, the function looks for executable (or for the first item in args) relative to cwd if the executable path is a relative path.\nL280: \nL281: Changed in version 3.6: cwd parameter accepts a cite56†path-like object on POSIX.\nL282: \nL283: Changed in version 3.7: cwd parameter accepts a cite56†path-like object on Windows.\nL284: Changed in version 3.8: cwd parameter accepts a bytes object on Windows.\nL285: \nL286: If restore_signals is true (the default) all signals that Python has set to SIG_IGN are restored to SIG_DFL in the child process before the exec. Currently this includes the SIGPIPE, SIGXFZ and SIGXFSZ signals. (POSIX only)\nL287: \nL288: Changed in version 3.2: restore_signals was added.\nL289: \nL290: If start_new_session is true the `setsid()` system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess.\nL291: \nL292: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL293: Changed in version 3.2: start_new_session was added.\nL294: \nL295: If process_group is a non-negative integer, the `setpgid(0, value)` system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess.\nL296: \nL297: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL298: \nL299: Changed in version 3.11: process_group was added.\nL300: If group is not `None`, the setregid() system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess. If the provided value is a string, it will be looked up via cite68†`grp.getgrnam()` and the value in `gr_gid` will be used. If the value is an integer, it will be passed verbatim. (POSIX only)\nL301: \nL302: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL303: \nL304: Added in version 3.9.\nL305: If extra_groups is not `None`, the setgroups() system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess. Strings provided in extra_groups will be looked up via cite68†`grp.getgrnam()` and the values in `gr_gid` will be used. Integer values will be passed verbatim. (POSIX only)\nL306: \nL307: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL308: \nL309: Added in version 3.9.\nL310: If user is not `None`, the setreuid() system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess. If the provided value is a string, it will be looked up via cite69†`pwd.getpwnam()` and the value in `pw_uid` will be used. If the value is an integer, it will be passed verbatim. (POSIX only)\nL311: \nL312: Note\nL313: Specifying user will not drop existing supplementary group memberships! The caller must also pass `extra_groups=()` to reduce the group membership of the child process for security purposes.\nL314: \nL315: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL316: \nL317: Added in version 3.9.\nL318: \nL319: If umask is not negative, the umask() system call will be made in the child process prior to the execution of the subprocess.\nL320: \nL321: cite3†Availability : POSIX\nL322: \nL323: Added in version 3.9.\nL324: If env is not `None`, it must be a mapping that defines the environment variables for the new process; these are used instead of the default behavior of inheriting the current process’ environment. This mapping can be str to str on any platform or bytes to bytes on POSIX platforms much like cite17†`os.environ` or cite18†`os.environb` .\nL325: \nL326: Note\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsubprocess — Subprocess management — Python 3.14.8 documentation (https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/subprocess.html)\nciteturn60view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn59view1\",\"pattern\":\"TimeoutExpired\"}); Total lines: 920\nL30: A timeout may be specified in seconds, it is internally passed on to cite13†`Popen.communicate()` . If the timeout expires, the child process will be killed and waited for. The cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exception will be re-raised after the child process has terminated. The initial process creation itself cannot be interrupted on many platform APIs so you are not guaranteed to see a timeout exception until at least after however long process creation takes.\nL96: Special value that can be used as the stdin, stdout or stderr argument to cite8†`Popen` and indicates that a pipe to the standard stream should be opened. Most useful with cite13†`Popen.communicate()` .\nL97: \nL98: subprocess.STDOUTcite12†¶ L99: \nL100: \nL101: Special value that can be used as the stderr argument to cite8†`Popen` and indicates that standard error should go into the same handle as standard output.\nL102: \nL103: exception subprocess.SubprocessErrorcite26†¶ L104: \nL105: \nL106: Base class for all other exceptions from this module.\nL107: \nL108: Added in version 3.3.\nL109: exception subprocess.TimeoutExpiredcite14†¶ L110: \nL111: \nL112: Subclass of cite26†`SubprocessError` , raised when a timeout expires while waiting for a child process.\nL113: \nL114: cmdcite27†¶ L115: \nL116: \nL117: Command that was used to spawn the child process.\nL118: \nL119: timeoutcite28†¶ L120: \nL121: \nL122: Timeout in seconds.\nL123: \nL124: outputcite29†¶ L125: Output of the child process if it was captured by cite7†`run()` or cite30†`check_output()` . Otherwise, `None`. This is always cite31†`bytes` when any output was captured regardless of the `text=True` setting. It may remain `None` instead of `b''` when no output was observed.\nL126: \nL127: stdoutcite32†¶ L128: \nL129: \nL130: Alias for output, for symmetry with cite33†`stderr` .\nL131: \nL132: stderrcite33†¶ L133: Stderr output of the child process if it was captured by cite7†`run()` . Otherwise, `None`. This is always cite31†`bytes` when stderr output was captured regardless of the `text=True` setting. It may remain `None` instead of `b''` when no stderr output was observed.\nL134: \nL135: Added in version 3.3.\nL136: \nL137: Changed in version 3.5: stdout and stderr attributes added\nL138: \nL139: exception subprocess.CalledProcessErrorcite15†¶ L379: A cite91†`ValueError` will be raised if cite8†`Popen` is called with invalid arguments.\nL380: \nL381: cite34†`check_call()` and cite30†`check_output()` will raise cite15†`CalledProcessError` if the called process returns a non-zero return code.\nL382: \nL383: All of the functions and methods that accept a timeout parameter, such as cite7†`run()` and cite13†`Popen.communicate()` will raise cite14†`TimeoutExpired` if the timeout expires before the process exits.\nL384: \nL385: Exceptions defined in this module all inherit from cite26†`SubprocessError` .\nL390: On Windows, batch files (`*.bat` or `*.cmd`) may be launched by the operating system in a system shell regardless of the arguments passed to this library. This could result in arguments being parsed according to shell rules, but without any escaping added by Python. If you are intentionally launching a batch file with arguments from untrusted sources, consider passing `shell=True` to allow Python to escape special characters. See cite95†gh-114539†github.com for additional discussion.\nL391: ## Popen Objectscite96†¶ L392: \nL393: Instances of the cite8†`Popen` class have the following methods:\nL394: \nL395: Popen.poll()cite97†¶ L396: \nL397: \nL398: Check if child process has terminated. Set and return cite88†`returncode` attribute. Otherwise, returns `None`.\nL399: \nL400: Popen.wait(timeout=None)cite98†¶ L401: \nL402: \nL403: Wait for child process to terminate. Set and return cite88†`returncode` attribute.\nL404: \nL405: If the process does not terminate after timeout seconds, raise a cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exception. It is safe to catch this exception and retry the wait.\nL406: \nL407: Note\nL408: This will deadlock when using `stdout=PIPE` or `stderr=PIPE` and the child process generates enough output to a pipe such that it blocks waiting for the OS pipe buffer to accept more data. Use cite13†`Popen.communicate()` when using pipes to avoid that.\nL409: \nL410: Note\nL411: \nL412: When the `timeout` parameter is not `None`, then (on POSIX) the function is implemented using a busy loop (non-blocking call and short sleeps). Use the cite99†`asyncio` module for an asynchronous wait: see cite100†`asyncio.create_subprocess_exec` .\nL413: Changed in version 3.3: timeout was added.\nL414: \nL415: Popen.communicate(input=None, timeout=None)cite13†¶ L416: \nL417: \nL418: Interact with process: Send data to stdin. Read data from stdout and stderr, until end-of-file is reached. Wait for process to terminate and set the cite88†`returncode` attribute. The optional input argument should be data to be sent to the child process, or `None`, if no data should be sent to the child. If streams were opened in text mode, input must be a string. Otherwise, it must be bytes.\nL419: `communicate()` returns a tuple `(stdout_data, stderr_data)`. The data will be strings if streams were opened in text mode; otherwise, bytes.\nL420: \nL421: Note that if you want to send data to the process’s stdin, you need to create the Popen object with `stdin=PIPE`. Similarly, to get anything other than `None` in the result tuple, you need to give `stdout=PIPE` and/or `stderr=PIPE` too.\nL422: If the process does not terminate after timeout seconds, a cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exception will be raised. Catching this exception and retrying communication will not lose any output. Supplying input to a subsequent post-timeout `communicate()` call is in undefined behavior and may become an error in the future.\nL423: The child process is not killed if the timeout expires, so in order to cleanup properly a well-behaved application should kill the child process and finish communication:\nL424: \nL425:     proc = subprocess.Popen(...)\nL426:     try:\nL427:         outs, errs = proc.communicate(timeout=15)\nL428:     except TimeoutExpired:\nL429:         proc.kill()\nL430:         outs, errs = proc.communicate()\nL431: After a call to `communicate()` raises cite14†`TimeoutExpired` , do not call cite98†`wait()` . Use an additional `communicate()` call to finish handling pipes and populate the cite88†`returncode` attribute.\nL432: \nL433: Note\nL434: \nL435: The data read is buffered in memory, so do not use this method if the data size is large or unlimited.\nL436: \nL437: Changed in version 3.3: timeout was added.\nL438: \nL439: Popen.send_signal(signal)cite101†¶ L440: \nL441: \nL442: Sends the signal signal to the child.\nL443: \nL444: Do nothing if the process completed.\nL445: \nL446: Note\nL447: On Windows, SIGTERM is an alias for cite102†`terminate()` . CTRL_C_EVENT and CTRL_BREAK_EVENT can be sent to processes started with a creationflags parameter which includes `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP`.\nL448: \nL449: Popen.terminate()cite102†¶ L450: \nL451: \nL452: Stop the child. On POSIX OSs the method sends cite103†`SIGTERM` to the child. On Windows the Win32 API function `TerminateProcess()` is called to stop the child.\nL453: \nL454: Popen.kill()cite104†¶ L455: Kills the child. On POSIX OSs the function sends SIGKILL to the child. On Windows `kill()` is an alias for cite102†`terminate()` .\nL456: \nL457: The following attributes are also set by the class for you to access. Reassigning them to new values is unsupported:\nL458: \nL459: Popen.argscite105†¶ L460: \nL461: \nL462: The args argument as it was passed to cite8†`Popen` – a sequence of program arguments or else a single string.\nL463: \nL464: Added in version 3.3.\nL465: \nL466: Popen.stdincite43†¶ L467: If the stdin argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a writeable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stdin argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL468: \nL469: Popen.stdoutcite44†¶ L470: If the stdout argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a readable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . Reading from the stream provides output from the child process. If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stdout argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL471: \nL472: Popen.stderrcite45†¶ L473: If the stderr argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a readable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . Reading from the stream provides error output from the child process. If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stderr argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL474: \nL475: Warning\nL880: ### Timeout Behaviorcite142†¶ L881: \nL882: When using the `timeout` parameter in functions like cite7†`run()` , cite98†`Popen.wait()` , or cite13†`Popen.communicate()` , users should be aware of the following behaviors:\nL883: \nL884:   1. Process Creation Delay: The initial process creation itself cannot be interrupted on many platform APIs. This means that even when specifying a timeout, you are not guaranteed to see a timeout exception until at least after however long process creation takes.\nL885:   2. Extremely Small Timeout Values: Setting very small timeout values (such as a few milliseconds) may result in almost immediate cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exceptions because process creation and system scheduling inherently require time.\nL886: ### Converting an argument sequence to a string on Windowscite143†¶ L887: \nL888: On Windows, an args sequence is converted to a string that can be parsed using the following rules (which correspond to the rules used by the MS C runtime):\nL889: \nL890:   1. Arguments are delimited by white space, which is either a space or a tab.\nL891: \nL892:   2. A string surrounded by double quotation marks is interpreted as a single argument, regardless of white space contained within. A quoted string can be embedded in an argument.\nL893:   3. A double quotation mark preceded by a backslash is interpreted as a literal double quotation mark.\nL894: \nL895:   4. Backslashes are interpreted literally, unless they immediately precede a double quotation mark.\nL896: \nL897:   5. If backslashes immediately precede a double quotation mark, every pair of backslashes is interpreted as a literal backslash. If the number of backslashes is odd, the last backslash escapes the next double quotation mark as described in rule 3.\nL898: \nL899: See also\nL900: \nL901: cite144†`shlex` -------------------------------------