cite88†`returncode` attribute.\nL404: \nL405: If the process does not terminate after timeout seconds, raise a cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exception. It is safe to catch this exception and retry the wait.\nL406: \nL407: Note\nL408: This will deadlock when using `stdout=PIPE` or `stderr=PIPE` and the child process generates enough output to a pipe such that it blocks waiting for the OS pipe buffer to accept more data. Use cite13†`Popen.communicate()` when using pipes to avoid that.\nL409: \nL410: Note\nL411: \nL412: When the `timeout` parameter is not `None`, then (on POSIX) the function is implemented using a busy loop (non-blocking call and short sleeps). Use the cite99†`asyncio` module for an asynchronous wait: see cite100†`asyncio.create_subprocess_exec` .\nL413: Changed in version 3.3: timeout was added.\nL414: \nL415: Popen.communicate(input=None, timeout=None)cite13†¶ L416: \nL417: \nL418: Interact with process: Send data to stdin. Read data from stdout and stderr, until end-of-file is reached. Wait for process to terminate and set the cite88†`returncode` attribute. The optional input argument should be data to be sent to the child process, or `None`, if no data should be sent to the child. If streams were opened in text mode, input must be a string. Otherwise, it must be bytes.\nL419: `communicate()` returns a tuple `(stdout_data, stderr_data)`. The data will be strings if streams were opened in text mode; otherwise, bytes.\nL420: \nL421: Note that if you want to send data to the process’s stdin, you need to create the Popen object with `stdin=PIPE`. Similarly, to get anything other than `None` in the result tuple, you need to give `stdout=PIPE` and/or `stderr=PIPE` too.\nL422: If the process does not terminate after timeout seconds, a cite14†`TimeoutExpired` exception will be raised. Catching this exception and retrying communication will not lose any output. Supplying input to a subsequent post-timeout `communicate()` call is in undefined behavior and may become an error in the future.\nL423: The child process is not killed if the timeout expires, so in order to cleanup properly a well-behaved application should kill the child process and finish communication:\nL424: \nL425:     proc = subprocess.Popen(...)\nL426:     try:\nL427:         outs, errs = proc.communicate(timeout=15)\nL428:     except TimeoutExpired:\nL429:         proc.kill()\nL430:         outs, errs = proc.communicate()\nL431: After a call to `communicate()` raises cite14†`TimeoutExpired` , do not call cite98†`wait()` . Use an additional `communicate()` call to finish handling pipes and populate the cite88†`returncode` attribute.\nL432: \nL433: Note\nL434: \nL435: The data read is buffered in memory, so do not use this method if the data size is large or unlimited.\nL436: \nL437: Changed in version 3.3: timeout was added.\nL438: \nL439: Popen.send_signal(signal)cite101†¶ L440: \nL441: \nL442: Sends the signal signal to the child.\nL443: \nL444: Do nothing if the process completed.\nL445: \nL446: Note\nL447: On Windows, SIGTERM is an alias for cite102†`terminate()` . CTRL_C_EVENT and CTRL_BREAK_EVENT can be sent to processes started with a creationflags parameter which includes `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP`.\nL448: \nL449: Popen.terminate()cite102†¶ L450: \nL451: \nL452: Stop the child. On POSIX OSs the method sends cite103†`SIGTERM` to the child. On Windows the Win32 API function `TerminateProcess()` is called to stop the child.\nL453: \nL454: Popen.kill()cite104†¶ L455: Kills the child. On POSIX OSs the function sends SIGKILL to the child. On Windows `kill()` is an alias for cite102†`terminate()` .\nL456: \nL457: The following attributes are also set by the class for you to access. Reassigning them to new values is unsupported:\nL458: \nL459: Popen.argscite105†¶ L460: \nL461: \nL462: The args argument as it was passed to cite8†`Popen` – a sequence of program arguments or else a single string.\nL463: \nL464: Added in version 3.3.\nL465: \nL466: Popen.stdincite43†¶ L467: If the stdin argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a writeable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stdin argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL468: \nL469: Popen.stdoutcite44†¶ L470: If the stdout argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a readable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . Reading from the stream provides output from the child process. If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stdout argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL471: \nL472: Popen.stderrcite45†¶ L473: If the stderr argument was cite11†`PIPE` , this attribute is a readable stream object as returned by cite61†`open()` . Reading from the stream provides error output from the child process. If the encoding or errors arguments were specified or the text or universal_newlines argument was `True`, the stream is a text stream, otherwise it is a byte stream. If the stderr argument was not `PIPE`, this attribute is `None`.\nL474: \nL475: Warning\nL476: Use cite13†`communicate()` rather than cite43†`.stdin.write` , cite44†`.stdout.read` or cite45†`.stderr.read` to avoid deadlocks due to any of the other OS pipe buffers filling up and blocking the child process.\nL477: \nL478: Popen.pidcite106†¶ L479: \nL480: \nL481: The process ID of the child process.\nL482: \nL483: Note that if you set the shell argument to `True`, this is the process ID of the spawned shell.\nL484: \nL485: Popen.returncodecite88†¶ L486: The child return code. Initially `None`, cite88†`returncode` is set by a call to the cite97†`poll()` , cite98†`wait()` , or cite13†`communicate()` methods if they detect that the process has terminated.\nL487: \nL488: A `None` value indicates that the process hadn’t yet terminated at the time of the last method call.\nL489: \nL490: A negative value `-N` indicates that the child was terminated by signal `N` (POSIX only).\nL491: When `shell=True`, the return code reflects the exit status of the shell itself (e.g. `/bin/sh`), which may map signals to codes such as `128+N`. See the documentation of the shell (for example, the Bash manual’s Exit Status) for details.\nL492: ## Windows Popen Helperscite107†¶ L493: \nL494: The cite71†`STARTUPINFO` class and following constants are only available on Windows.\nL495: \nL496: class subprocess.STARTUPINFO(* (Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)), dwFlags=0, hStdInput=None, hStdOutput=None, hStdError=None, wShowWindow=0, lpAttributeList=None)cite71†¶ L497: \nL498: \nL499: Partial support of the Windows cite108†STARTUPINFO†msdn.microsoft.com structure is used for cite8†`Popen` creation. The following attributes can be set by passing them as keyword-only arguments.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmath — Mathematical functions — Python 3.14.8 documentation (https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/math.html)\nciteturn60view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn59view0\",\"pattern\":\"math.pi\"}); Total lines: 550\nL461: ## Special functionscite87†¶ L462: \nL463: math.erf(x)cite55†¶ L464: \nL465: \nL466: Return the cite56†error function†en.wikipedia.org at x.\nL467: \nL468: The `erf()` function can be used to compute traditional statistical functions such as the cite88†cumulative standard normal distribution†en.wikipedia.org :\nL469: \nL470:     def phi(x):\nL471:         'Cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution'\nL472:         return (1.0 + erf(x / sqrt(2.0))) / 2.0\nL473: \nL474: \nL475: Added in version 3.2.\nL476: \nL477: math.erfc(x)cite57†¶ L478: Return the complementary error function at x. The cite56†complementary error function†en.wikipedia.org is defined as `1.0 - erf(x)`. It is used for large values of x where a subtraction from one would cause a cite78†loss of significance†en.wikipedia.org .\nL479: \nL480: Added in version 3.2.\nL481: \nL482: math.gamma(x)cite58†¶ L483: \nL484: \nL485: Return the cite59†Gamma function†en.wikipedia.org at x.\nL486: \nL487: Added in version 3.2.\nL488: \nL489: math.lgamma(x)cite60†¶ L490: \nL491: \nL492: Return the natural logarithm of the absolute value of the Gamma function at x.\nL493: \nL494: Added in version 3.2.\nL495: ## Constantscite89†¶ L496: \nL497: math.picite61†¶ L498: \nL499: \nL500: The mathematical constant π = 3.141592…, to available precision.\nL501: \nL502: math.ecite62†¶ L503: \nL504: \nL505: The mathematical constant e = 2.718281…, to available precision.\nL506: \nL507: math.taucite63†¶ L508: The mathematical constant τ = 6.283185…, to available precision. Tau is a circle constant equal to 2π, the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its radius. To learn more about Tau, check out Vi Hart’s video cite90†Pi is (still) Wrong†vimeo.com , and start celebrating cite91†Tau day†tauday.com by eating twice as much pie!\nL509: \nL510: Added in version 3.6.\nL511: \nL512: math.infcite64†¶ L513: \nL514: \nL515: A floating-point positive infinity. (For negative infinity, use `-math.inf`.) Equivalent to the output of `float('inf')`.\nL516: \nL517: Added in version 3.5.\nL518: math.nancite65†¶ L519: A floating-point “not a number” (NaN) value. Equivalent to the output of `float('nan')`. Due to the requirements of the cite92†IEEE-754 standard†en.wikipedia.org , `math.nan` and `float('nan')` are not considered to equal to any other numeric value, including themselves. To check whether a number is a NaN, use the cite21†`isnan()` function to test for NaNs instead of `is` or `==`. Example:\nL520: \nL521:     >>> import math\nL522:     >>> math.nan == math.nan\nL523:     False\nL524:     >>> float('nan') == float('nan')\nL525:     False\nL526:     >>> math.isnan(math.nan)\nL527:     True\nL528:     >>> math.isnan(float('nan'))\nL529:     True\nL530: Added in version 3.5.\nL531: \nL532: Changed in version 3.11: It is now always available.\nL533: CPython implementation detail: The `math` module consists mostly of thin wrappers around the platform C math library functions. Behavior in exceptional cases follows Annex F of the C99 standard where appropriate. The current implementation will raise cite68†`ValueError` for invalid operations like `sqrt(-1.0)` or `log(0.0)` (where C99 Annex F recommends signaling invalid operation or divide-by-zero), and cite93†`OverflowError` for results that overflow (for example, `exp(1000.0)`).\n"
